        "num": "000",
        "name": "Entrance Hall",
        "html": "<p>The entryway is a vast, two-story hall paneled from floor to ceiling in dark, heavily carved wood. Behind the players stand the two massive halves of the front entrance, each fitted with black iron hinges, a long brass handle, and narrow panes of glass. Directly ahead, a broad staircase rises beneath a pointed arch, its red carpet held in place by tarnished brass rods. The stairs divide at the upper landing and join a gallery that overlooks the hall through a row of narrow Gothic arches. Two elaborate brass candelabras stand on heavy sculpted bases at the foot of the staircase, each holding dozens of tall, unlit candles. Eight interior doors are arranged symmetrically around the hall. Three line the left wall and three line the right. The remaining pair face back into the entryway from beneath the staircase, one on either side, and appear to be matching closets built into the paneling. Each door stands within an ornate pointed arch and is made from the same dark wood as the walls, making the closed ones nearly disappear. Seven are shut. The farthest door along the left wall stands open onto a long Gothic gallery.  <span class=\"hidden-detail\">[The open door may be the only one that moves easily when the players first arrive. The others may be locked, stuck, or simply open onto different rooms when tried later. The two doors beneath the stairs may be ordinary closets, passages into the walls, or something else entirely.]</span> An umbrella stand beside the entrance holds several black umbrellas, and a small reception table bears a silver calling-card tray, a handbell, and a guest ledger opened to a blank page. <span class=\"hidden-detail\">[The ledger already contains the visitors\u2019 names on earlier pages, written in different inks and dated across many years.]</span> Above the front doors hangs a large carved crest depicting a house beneath a gallows tree. The face of the clock set into its trunk has no hands, though a slow ticking can be heard somewhere in the hall.</p><p>The gallary: Heavy ribs divide its vaulted ceiling into repeating arches, supported by carved wooden columns that continue into the distance. Tall leaded windows line the left side, their panes washed grey by the rain outside. Dim wall sconces repeat between the columns on both sides, lighting console tables, closed doors, and large paintings whose subjects cannot be made out from the entryway. Their light creates alternating bands of amber and shadow across the patterned wooden floor. At the far end, an arched doorway is barely visible through the darkness, much farther away than the dimensions of the house should allow.</p>",
